<h1>Video 069 — Constructors (default + overloading)</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Constructors in java | default constructor | Constructor Overloading</p>
<p>ASR decode (Whisper): role / rolambar / rule number = roll number; name is 4 / Sir Nal / Nal = null; Varychev Vatnam = Telugu “what is your name”; this is our / this is time = this.name; meta button / neither button = method but not; written / retent / wide = return type / void; FFP = final/static/…; no RG / no arc / no RK / newer = no-arg; so far / so per / sofa / sober = super; this (with paren sense) = this(); P-scode / Peace Code = programmer’s code; C-scode = compiler-generated code; Yamman / Yemen = m1; Ivo / IBO = IOException; TriCatch = try-catch; Cheyle / chilled / cellulaclass = child; customizer = customized; ENT room = interview room; Java C = javac; keep subiting = keep writing notes.</p>
<p>Overlap note: Content heavily overlaps playlist videos 064 (constructors intro/rules), 065 (default constructor), and 066 (overloaded constructors). This lecture is a longer standalone / compilation-style pass — board work is still documented fully here.</p>
<h2>Lecture notes</h2>
<h3>00:11 — Why constructors? Student without initialization</h3>
<p>After OO features (including typecasting), next exam-heavy topic: constructors. Expect roughly 2–4 certification questions from this area.</p>
<p>Board: every student must have name and roll number.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNumber;
}</pre>
<p>Creating many objects without assigning values:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNumber;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student();
        Student s2 = new Student();
        // … imagine 600 Student objects
    }
}</pre>
<p>For every object, JVM creates a separate copy of instance variables and fills default values:</p>
<ul><li>name (String) → null</li></ul>
<ul><li>rollNumber (int) → 0</li></ul>
<p>Approaching any of 600 students: “my name is null, roll number is 0” — meaningless. Object creation without initialization is not useful service.</p>
<p>Meaningful objects need distinct values, e.g. Durga/101, Ravi/102, Shiva/103. Initialization of instance variables is compulsory for meaningful objects.</p>
<h3>06:10 — Who initializes? Purpose of constructor</h3>
<p>A special concept is required for initialization: the constructor.</p>
<ul><li>Purpose: perform initialization of an object (not create the object).</li></ul>
<ul><li>After object creation, constructor runs immediately to initialize.</li></ul>
<p>First sketch of a Student constructor (rules detailed later):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNumber;

    Student(String name, int rollNumber) {
        this.name = name;
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
    }
}</pre>
<p>Rules preview: constructor name = class name; parameters match what must be initialized; this.name = name assigns argument into instance field. IDEs (Eclipse / NetBeans) can generate constructors; syntax is fixed.</p>
<h3>09:16 — Creating objects with constructor args</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNumber;

    Student(String name, int rollNumber) {
        this.name = name;
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student(&quot;Durga&quot;, 101);
    }
}</pre>
<p>Misconception killed: “Constructor’s main purpose is to create the object.” Wrong.</p>
<ul><li>Constructor job = initialize.</li></ul>
<ul><li>`new` creates the object.</li></ul>
<h3>10:39 — Full story of one `new` line</h3>
<p>For Student s1 = new Student(&quot;Durga&quot;, 101); three activities:</p>
<ol><li>Create Student object (new).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Perform initialization (constructor).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Hold reference in s1.</li></ol>
<p>Who creates the object? `new`, not the constructor.</p>
<p>Because of new:</p>
<ol><li>Object created; separate copies of name, rollNumber.</li></ol>
<ol><li>JVM assigns defaults: name = null, rollNumber = 0.</li></ol>
<p>Constructor has not started yet. Then the constructor call Student(&quot;Durga&quot;, 101) runs: arguments bind; this.name = name replaces null with &quot;Durga&quot;; this.rollNumber = rollNumber replaces 0 with 101.</p>
<p>this = current object (the one on which this constructor is executing).</p>
<p>Second object:</p>
<pre class='code'>Student s2 = new Student(&quot;Ravi&quot;, 102);</pre>
<p>Again: new → defaults null/0 → constructor replaces with Ravi/102.</p>
<p>Purpose of constructor: perform initialization of an object — not create it. Creation = new.</p>
<h3>18:21 — Executable Student demo</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Student {
    String name;
    int rollNumber;

    Student(String name, int rollNumber) {
        this.name = name;
        this.rollNumber = rollNumber;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s1 = new Student(&quot;Durga&quot;, 101);
        Student s2 = new Student(&quot;Ravi&quot;, 102);
        System.out.println(s1.name + &quot; &quot; + s1.rollNumber);
        System.out.println(s2.name + &quot; &quot; + s2.rollNumber);
    }
}
// javac Student.java &amp;&amp; java Student
// prints Durga 101
// prints Ravi 102</pre>
<p>Two objects → constructor executes twice, once per object.</p>
<h3>21:37 — How many times does a constructor run?</h3>
<p>Constructor runs after object creation (new), once per object. Create 10 objects → constructor runs 10 times.</p>
<p>Demo (print only — no real field init):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;constructor&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        Test t2 = new Test();
        Test t3 = new Test();
    }
}
// prints constructor
// prints constructor
// prints constructor</pre>
<p>Add Test t4 = new Test(); → four prints. Syntax familiarity + “once per object” is the point.</p>
<h3>26:00 — Rule 1: name of constructor = name of class</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        // constructor
    }
}</pre>
<p>Name of constructor and name of class must be same.</p>
<h3>27:26 — Rule 2: return type not applicable (even void)</h3>
<p>Constructor executes automatically on new — we do not call it like a method. Therefore return type concept is not applicable, including void.</p>
<p>Trap: if you write void by mistake, compiler treats it as a method, not a constructor.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    void Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        Test t2 = new Test();
    }
}
// compiles and runs
// prints (nothing) — void Test() is a method; never auto-run on new</pre>
<p>If it were a constructor, “Hello” would print twice. It does not → proof it is a method.</p>
<p>Calling the method explicitly:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    void Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test();
        t1.Test(); // method call
    }
}
// prints Hello</pre>
<p>Legal but not recommended: a method whose name equals the class name is allowed (this is the example), but it is stupid / bad practice — confuses readers who expect a constructor.</p>
<p>Rules so far:</p>
<ol><li>Constructor name = class name.</li></ol>
<ol><li>No return type (not even void); with return type → method, not constructor.</li></ol>
<h3>35:14 — Which modifiers apply to constructors?</h3>
<p>Only these four:</p>
<ul><li>public</li></ul>
<ul><li>default (no modifier)</li></ul>
<ul><li>protected</li></ul>
<ul><li>private</li></ul>
<p>Not allowed: final, static, strictfp, abstract, native, synchronized, … → CE (“modifier … not allowed here”).</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public Test() { }      // valid
    // private Test() { }  // also valid
    // protected Test() { }// also valid
    // Test() { }          // default — valid
}
class Test {
    final Test() { }
}
// CE: modifier final not allowed here
class Test {
    static Test() { }
}
// CE: modifier static not allowed here
class Test {
    synchronized Test() { }
}
// CE: modifier synchronized not allowed here</pre>
<p>Access meaning (high level):</p>
<p>Private constructors are used for singleton classes (only one object allowed) — e.g. patterns around DateFormat / NumberFormat discussed later in advanced / framework context. Video 067 covers singleton in depth.</p>
<h3>40:41 — Default constructor</h3>
<p>Empty class — does it contain a constructor?</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
}</pre>
<p>Yes. Programmer did not write one; compiler adds a default constructor at compile time.</p>
<ul><li>Every class in Java (including abstract classes) contains a constructor concept. No class exists without a constructor.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Who generates default constructor? Compiler, not JVM. (Some books/sites wrongly say JVM.)</li></ul>
<ul><li>When? Only if the class contains no constructor at all. If you write at least one constructor, compiler does not add default.</li></ul>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test(int i) {
    }
}
// no default constructor generated — you already wrote one</pre>
<p>Default and customized constructors cannot both exist in the same class: if you write any constructor, default is not generated; if you write none, default is generated.</p>
<p>Abstract class can contain constructor? Yes — every class must have constructor concept.</p>
<h3>46:22 — Prototype of default constructor (3 rules)</h3>
<ol><li>Default constructor is always a no-arg constructor.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Access modifier of default constructor = same as class modifier (for public / default). If class is public → public Test(); if class is default → package-private Test(). Top-level class cannot be private/protected, so those cases do not arise. final/static etc. are not constructor modifiers.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Default constructor contains only one line: super(); — no-arg call to superclass constructor.</li></ol>
<p>Important distinction:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>This is a no-arg constructor you wrote yourself — not a default constructor. Default = only the one generated by the compiler.</p>
<p>Default constructor is always no-arg, but every no-arg constructor is not a default constructor.</p>
<p>Prototype sketch (compiler-generated, default class):</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<h3>52:18 — Programmer code (P) vs compiler code (C) — table of cases</h3>
<p>P-code = what the programmer writes. C-code = what the compiler generates.</p>
<h4>Case A — empty default class</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
class Test {
}
// C-code (conceptual after compilation)
class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Case B — public empty class</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
public class Test {
}
// C-code
public class Test {
    public Test() {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Case C — method named like class (void) → still no real constructor written</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
class Test {
    void Test() {
    }
}
// C-code — default constructor still added; method stays
class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
    }

    void Test() {
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Case D — programmer wrote no-arg constructor (no first-line call)</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
class Test {
    Test() {
    }
}</pre>
<p>Compiler does not add a second default constructor. But it still ensures first line is super or this:</p>
<pre class='code'>// C-code
class Test {
    Test() {
        super(); // inserted by compiler
    }
}</pre>
<p>Rule: first line inside every constructor must be either super(...) or this(...). If programmer writes neither, compiler inserts super();.</p>
<h4>Case E — overloaded ctors; one uses `this()`, other empty body</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        this();
    }

    Test() {
    }
}
// C-code
class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        this();
    }

    Test() {
        super(); // inserted
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Case F — programmer already wrote `super()`</h4>
<pre class='code'>// P-code
class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        super();
    }
}
// C-code — same (compiler generates nothing extra)
class Test {
    Test(int i) {
        super();
    }
}</pre>
<p>Summary from discussion:</p>
<ul><li>Default constructor only if no constructor written.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Prototype: no-arg; access = class access; body = super();.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Every constructor’s first line must be super or this; else compiler places super();.</li></ul>
<h3>1:05:06 — Case studies: `super` / `this` as constructor calls</h3>
<h4>Case 1 — `super()` not first statement → CE</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;constructor&quot;);
        super(); // CE: not first statement
    }
}
// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>Fix: put super(); first.</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
        System.out.println(&quot;constructor&quot;);
    }
}</pre>
<h4>Case 2 — both `super()` and `this()` → CE</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        super();
        this(); // CE — not first; cannot use both
        System.out.println(&quot;constructor&quot;);
    }
}
// CE: call to this must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>You may use either super or this, only as the first statement, not both.</p>
<h4>Case 3 — `super()` inside a method → CE</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public void m1() {
        super(); // CE — not in a constructor
        System.out.println(&quot;method&quot;);
    }
}
// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>super() / this() call constructors only from constructors, not from ordinary methods. You can call a constructor directly only from another constructor.</p>
<h3>1:19:06 — Rules for `super()` / `this()` constructor calls</h3>
<p>Exam loophole territory.</p>
<h3>1:21:00 — `super` / `this` as **keywords** (not calls)</h3>
<p>Different from super() / this():</p>
<ul><li>Constructor calls: super(...), this(...) — call parent / current-class constructors.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Keywords: super.x, this.x, super.m1(), this.m1() — refer to superclass / current-class instance members.</li></ul>
<p>Parent and child both have String s:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    String s = &quot;parent variable&quot;;
}

class C extends P {
    String s = &quot;child variable&quot;;

    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(s);        // child variable (default)
        System.out.println(this.s);   // child variable
        System.out.println(super.s);  // parent variable
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        c.m1();
    }
}
// prints child variable
// prints child variable
// prints parent variable</pre>
<p>Keywords usable anywhere (methods, constructors), any number of times, together — but not in static context (they talk about objects / instance members).</p>
<pre class='code'>class C extends P {
    String s = &quot;child variable&quot;;

    public static void m1() {
        System.out.println(this.s);  // CE
        System.out.println(super.s); // CE
    }
}
// CE: non-static variable this cannot be referenced from a static context
// CE: non-static variable super cannot be referenced from a static context</pre>
<h3>1:30:03 — Table: constructor calls vs keywords</h3>
<h3>1:32:26 — Constructor overloading (need + demo)</h3>
<p>College inquiry system: different students provide different amounts of data → multiple constructors in same class (full data / without mobile / name only…).</p>
<ul><li>A class may contain any number of constructors.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Same name (class name), different argument lists → overloading.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Overloading is applicable to constructors (interview question).</li></ul>
<p>Board demo with chaining via this():</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test(double d) {
        this(10);
        System.out.println(&quot;double-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test(int i) {
        this();
        System.out.println(&quot;int-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;no-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t1 = new Test(10.5);
        // Test t2 = new Test(10);
        // Test t3 = new Test();
    }
}</pre>
<p>new Test(10.5) → double-arg ctor → this(10) → int-arg ctor → this() → no-arg ctor. Prints finish in reverse chain order:</p>
<pre class='code'>// prints no-arg constructor
// prints int-arg constructor
// prints double-arg constructor
class Test {
    Test(double d) {
        this(10);
        System.out.println(&quot;double-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test(int i) {
        this();
        System.out.println(&quot;int-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;no-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t2 = new Test(10);
    }
}
// prints no-arg constructor
// prints int-arg constructor
class Test {
    Test(double d) {
        this(10);
        System.out.println(&quot;double-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test(int i) {
        this();
        System.out.println(&quot;int-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    Test() {
        System.out.println(&quot;no-arg constructor&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t3 = new Test();
    }
}
// prints no-arg constructor</pre>
<p>Corresponding constructor is chosen by arguments; this(...) chains to another constructor of the same class.</p>
<h3>1:44:20 — Inheritance / overriding **not** applicable to constructors</h3>
<ul><li>Overloading: yes for constructors.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Inheritance / overriding: no for constructors (only for methods).</li></ul>
<p>Methods inherit:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    public void m1() {
        System.out.println(&quot;m1&quot;);
    }
}

class C extends P {
    public void m2() {
        System.out.println(&quot;m2&quot;);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        C c = new C();
        c.m1(); // inherited
        c.m2();
    }
}
// prints m1
// prints m2</pre>
<p>Parent may override m1 in child if needed — inheritance + overriding apply to methods.</p>
<p>Constructors do not inherit:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    P() {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    C(int i) {
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // C c = new C(); // CE — no no-arg ctor in C; P&#x27;s ctor not inherited
        C c = new C(10);
    }
}
// if uncommented: CE — constructor C in class C cannot be applied to given types
// required: int; found: no arguments</pre>
<p>Child has only the constructors it declares (plus default if none). Parent constructors are not available on the child type as inherited members → no overriding either (nothing to override).</p>
<h3>1:51:19 — Where can we write constructors?</h3>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
    }
}
// valid — concrete class
abstract class Test {
    int x;

    Test(int x) {
        this.x = x;
    }
}
// valid — abstract class constructor initializes instance state for children</pre>
<p>interface Test {
    Test() {
    }
}</p>
<pre class='code'>// CE — interfaces cannot have constructors (illegal start of type / similar)
interface Test {
    int x; // also invalid without initializer — must be public static final and initialized
}
// CE — interface fields must be initialized (static final)</pre>
<h3>1:58:00 — Case study: recursive **method** call vs recursive **constructor** call</h3>
<h4>Methods: no stack overflow until you ignite</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void m1() {
        m2();
    }

    public static void m2() {
        m1();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;);
    }
}
// prints Hello
// (m1/m2 never called — like matchbox + petrol without ignition)</pre>
<p>Ignite:</p>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    public static void m1() {
        m2();
    }

    public static void m2() {
        m1();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        m1();
    }
}
// compiles fine
// RE: java.lang.StackOverflowError</pre>
<p>Recursive method invocation → runtime StackOverflowError (no compile error).</p>
<h4>Constructors: recursive possibility is CE even if unused</h4>
<pre class='code'>class Test {
    Test() {
        this(10);
    }

    Test(int i) {
        this();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(&quot;Hello&quot;); // no object created
    }
}
// CE: recursive constructor invocation</pre>
<p>Compiler is strict about constructors: if recursive constructor invocation is possible, it is a compile-time error — whether or not you create an object.</p>
<h3>2:07:23 — Parent arg-constructor vs child’s implicit `super()`</h3>
<p>Three situations:</p>
<p>1 — both empty → valid (both get default super(); Object has no-arg).</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
}

class C extends P {
}
// valid</pre>
<p>2 — parent has explicit no-arg; child empty → valid (child’s default calls super(); parent has matching no-arg).</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    P() {
    }
}

class C extends P {
}
// valid</pre>
<p>3 — parent has only int-arg ctor; child empty → invalid.</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    P(int i) {
    }
}

class C extends P {
}
// CE: constructor P in class P cannot be applied to given types
// required: int; found: no arguments</pre>
<p>Internal story: child gets default constructor with super();, but parent has no no-arg constructor → CE.</p>
<p>Fixes:</p>
<p>Explicit child ctor calling matching parent:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    P(int i) {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    C() {
        super(10);
    }
}
// valid</pre>
<p>Or (recommended): whenever you write an argument constructor in a class, also write a no-arg constructor so children’s implicit super() still matches:</p>
<pre class='code'>class P {
    P() {
    }

    P(int i) {
    }
}

class C extends P {
}
// valid — child&#x27;s default super() finds P()</pre>
<h3>2:17:42 — Parent constructor `throws` checked exception</h3>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class P {
    P() throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
}
// CE: unreported exception IOException in default constructor</pre>
<p>Child’s compiler-generated default calls super(). Parent’s constructor throws checked IOException; the caller (child ctor) must handle it — but default ctor has neither try-catch nor throws → CE.</p>
<p>Reminder (exceptions): throws delegates handling to the caller. Caller must use try-catch or declare throws (same exception or parent type).</p>
<p>Can child wrap super() in try-catch?</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class P {
    P() throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    C() {
        try {
            super(); // CE — super must be first statement; cannot be inside try
        } catch (IOException e) {
        }
    }
}
// CE: call to super must be first statement in constructor</pre>
<p>Only workable approach: child constructor must `throws` the same checked exception or its parent:</p>
<pre class='code'>import java.io.*;

class P {
    P() throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    C() throws IOException {
        super();
    }
}
// valid
import java.io.*;

class P {
    P() throws IOException {
    }
}

class C extends P {
    C() throws Exception {
        super();
    }
}
// valid — Exception is parent of IOException</pre>
<p>Rule: if parent constructor throws a checked exception, every child constructor must throw the same checked exception or its parent type; try-catch around super() will not work because super/this must be the first statement.</p>
<h3>Closing takeaways (exam / interview)</h3>
<ol><li>Constructor initializes; `new` creates.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Name = class name; no return type (void → method).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Allowed modifiers: public / protected / private / default.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Default constructor: compiler-generated only if no ctor written; no-arg; access = class access; body super();.</li></ol>
<ol><li>First line of every ctor: super(...) or this(...) (else compiler inserts super();).</li></ol>
<ol><li>super()/this() vs super/this keywords — different rules.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Overloading yes; inheritance/overriding no.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Abstract class: ctor OK; interface: ctor not OK.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Recursive methods → runtime SOE; recursive constructors → CE.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Parent arg-only ctor / parent throws checked → child must take special care with constructors.</li></ol>
